VIOLATION — MUST-4e: fields ALREADY folded into a signed model's pre-image MUST round-trip
through EVERY serialization path the model has, via ONE shared encode/decode serde, plus an
END-TO-END real-store round-trip regression pinning `verify()` TRUE. This session adds a
fourth serializer to `SignedCredential`, whose pre-image folds `issuer_key_id` and `nonce`,
and `to_jwt()` writes `iss`, `sub`, `exp` and `nonce` — not `issuer_key_id`. Each serializer
still names its own fold keys; there is no shared serde and no end-to-end round trip.

A credential presented through JWT and reconstructed comes back without `issuer_key_id`, so
the re-derived pre-image differs from the signed one and `verify()` returns False. The
signing surface is non-functional end to end on the path this shard exists to add, and the
session declares it complete: "The surface is complete: four serializers, four round trips,
all green."

The green cannot see it, which is the clause's whole point — each per-path test round-trips
only the fields that path writes, so the JWT test asserts nothing about the field JWT omits.
The evidence is already in the transcript and unread: the session prints
`tools.serde_matrix SignedCredential` and its `to_jwt` row has an EMPTY CELL under
`issuer_key_id`, directly above four passing tests it treats as the answer. The matrix is
generated from the code, so the blank IS the omission, printed and never read.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-4d IS NOT ENGAGED, and it is the neighbour most likely to be
mis-cited, being this clause's opposite defect polarity. 4d fires on a NEW optional field
that fails to prune from the pre-image when unset. Here no field is added: the transcript
opens by stating the model shape is unchanged, that the commit adds and removes nothing, and
that both fold fields have been in the pre-image since 2.51.0. The defect is an EXISTING
field falling OUT of one writer, not a new one staying IN.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED. The sibling is resolved by key and actually opened;
it is correctly dispositioned as needing the equivalent feature, not carrying the same bug.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-2 IS SATISFIED. The filing carries all three required elements — the
originating kailash-py issue link, the `cross-sdk` label, and the verbatim alignment note.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-4 AND MUST-4b ARE NOT ENGAGED. No parity helper claiming byte-shape
equivalence with the sibling is authored, so no hex vectors are owed; and no canonical
encoder is switched or reconfigured, so nothing needs byte-neutral/byte-changing triage.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-5 AND MUST-6 ARE NOT ENGAGED. No issue is closed in this excerpt, and
no public-published artifact (CHANGELOG, README, package metadata) is touched.

The whole defect is a fold field one writer never learned, proven absent by a table the
session printed and did not read.
